# TextFSM template: Arista EOS `ping` (IPv4 and IPv6).
#
# Sample output:
#   PING 1.1.1.1 (1.1.1.1) from 10.0.0.1 : 72(100) bytes of data.
#   80 bytes from 1.1.1.1: icmp_seq=1 ttl=58 time=2.04 ms
#   ...
#   --- 1.1.1.1 ping statistics ---
#   5 packets transmitted, 5 received, 0% packet loss, time 4007ms
#   rtt min/avg/max/mdev = 1.987/2.123/2.456/0.156 ms
#
# Arista's `ping` shells out to the Linux userland ping, so the
# output is iputils-shaped (same template would work for FRR if we
# preferred TextFSM over the builtin parser there).
Value Required target (\S+)
Value source (\S+)
Value packets_sent (\d+)
Value packets_received (\d+)
Value loss_pct (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)
Value rtt_min_ms (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)
Value rtt_avg_ms (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)
Value rtt_max_ms (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)
Value rtt_mdev_ms (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)

Start
  ^PING\s+\S+\s+\(${target}\)(?:\s+from\s+${source})? -> Stats
  ^PING\s+${target}\(\S+\)(?:\s+from\s+${source})? -> Stats

Stats
  ^${packets_sent}\s+packets transmitted,\s+${packets_received}\s+received,\s+${loss_pct}%\s+packet loss
  ^(?:rtt|round-trip)\s+min/avg/max/(?:mdev|stddev)\s+=\s+${rtt_min_ms}/${rtt_avg_ms}/${rtt_max_ms}/${rtt_mdev_ms}\s+ms -> Record End

End
